{"componentChunkName":"component---src-pages-markdown-remark-frontmatter-slug-js","path":"/projects/scalable-risk-averse-well-placement/","result":{"data":{"markdownRemark":{"html":"<h1>Scalable Risk-Averse Well-Placement Optimization Using Quadratic Knapsack + Randomized SVD</h1>\n<p><strong>R. Farell, J. E. Bickel, C. Bajaj</strong></p>\n<p>Operations Research &#x26; Industrial Engineering, Oden Institute, and Department of Computer Science, The University of Texas at Austin</p>\n<p><strong>SPE Journal (2026)</strong>   |   <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.2118/231170-PA\">Paper (SPE 231170-PA)</a>   |   <a href=\"https://github.com/rfarell/well-placement-qkp\">Code</a></p>\n<hr>\n<h2>TL;DR</h2>\n<p>This work frames risk-averse shale well selection as a binary quadratic knapsack problem (QKP) under a CARA utility model, then accelerates it with randomized SVD (RSVD). On synthetic field-scale cases, low-rank RSVD surrogates deliver <strong>10x to 1,851x speedups</strong> while keeping certainty-equivalent (CE) error in the <strong>0.1% to 1%</strong> range, with <strong>&#x3C;=0.3% CE error at rank <code>r=100</code></strong> across tested kernels.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Operators must choose a small number of wells from thousands of candidates under strong spatial correlation and uncertainty in NPV. Naive expected-value optimization ignores risk concentration, while exact risk-aware quadratic optimization becomes expensive when covariance matrices are large.</p>\n<p><span class='gatsby-resp-image-wrapper' style='position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 800px; '>\n      <a class='gatsby-resp-image-link' href='/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/7e35d/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg' style='display: block' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>\n    <span class='gatsby-resp-image-background-image' style=\"padding-bottom: 27%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/jpeg;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"></span>\n  <img class='gatsby-resp-image-image' alt='Gaussian-process posterior (expected NPV and uncertainty)' title='' src='/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/4b190/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg' srcset='/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/e07e9/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg 200w,\n/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/066f9/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg 400w,\n/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/4b190/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg 800w,\n/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/e5166/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg 1200w,\n/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/b17f8/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg 1600w,\n/static/0b19a06dd4dba4f3a829646546f6fcad/7e35d/fig3_gp_posterior.jpg 1728w' sizes='(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px' style='width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;' loading='lazy' decoding='async'>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 3.</strong> GP posterior maps used to construct candidate-level expected NPV and uncertainty.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Method</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Build geostatistical mean vector <code>m</code> and covariance <code>C</code> over candidate wells.</li>\n<li>Optimize CARA-normal CE objective as a <code>0-1</code> QKP: maximize <code>m^T x - (lambda/2) x^T C x</code> with a cardinality cap.</li>\n<li>Replace dense <code>C</code> with RSVD low-rank surrogate <code>C ~= V V^T</code> to reduce per-node evaluation from <code>O(N^2)</code> to <code>O(Nr)</code>.</li>\n<li>Use error bounds to connect CE approximation quality to spectral tail decay and risk parameter <code>lambda</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class='gatsby-resp-image-wrapper' style='position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 800px; '>\n      <a class='gatsby-resp-image-link' href='/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/0f6e2/fig1_workflow.jpg' style='display: block' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>\n    <span class='gatsby-resp-image-background-image' style=\"padding-bottom: 27.999999999999996%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/jpeg;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"></span>\n  <img class='gatsby-resp-image-image' alt='End-to-end workflow: geostatistics -&gt; QKP -&gt; RSVD-accelerated solve' title='' src='/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/4b190/fig1_workflow.jpg' srcset='/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/e07e9/fig1_workflow.jpg 200w,\n/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/066f9/fig1_workflow.jpg 400w,\n/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/4b190/fig1_workflow.jpg 800w,\n/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/e5166/fig1_workflow.jpg 1200w,\n/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/b17f8/fig1_workflow.jpg 1600w,\n/static/d2e7bcb7fcf1428456f421ecd5004dd9/0f6e2/fig1_workflow.jpg 1716w' sizes='(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px' style='width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;' loading='lazy' decoding='async'>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Three-stage workflow: geostatistical inputs, QKP formulation, and RSVD acceleration.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Results</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Across tested synthetic scenarios (<code>N &#x3C;= 5,000</code>), RSVD-QKP reports <strong>10x-1,851x</strong> speedups.</li>\n<li>For practical ranks (<code>r >= 50</code>), CE gaps stay <strong>below 1%</strong> across tested kernels and risk levels.</li>\n<li>At <code>r=100</code>, CE gaps are <strong>&#x3C;=0.3%</strong> across tested kernels.</li>\n<li>In a challenging Matern case, <code>r=100</code> is about <strong>90x faster</strong> than dense QKP with around <strong>0.05% CE gap</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class='gatsby-resp-image-wrapper' style='position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 800px; '>\n      <a class='gatsby-resp-image-link' href='/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/32657/fig2_summary.jpg' style='display: block' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>\n    <span class='gatsby-resp-image-background-image' style=\"padding-bottom: 75.49999999999999%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/jpeg;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"></span>\n  <img class='gatsby-resp-image-image' alt='Cross-kernel RSVD performance summary' title='' src='/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/4b190/fig2_summary.jpg' srcset='/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/e07e9/fig2_summary.jpg 200w,\n/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/066f9/fig2_summary.jpg 400w,\n/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/4b190/fig2_summary.jpg 800w,\n/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/e5166/fig2_summary.jpg 1200w,\n/static/5d58dc9fc293babb170f706684be6dde/32657/fig2_summary.jpg 1372w' sizes='(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px' style='width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;' loading='lazy' decoding='async'>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 2.</strong> Speedup and CE-error summary across Matern, Exponential, and RBF kernels.</p>\n<p><span class='gatsby-resp-image-wrapper' style='position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 800px; '>\n      <a class='gatsby-resp-image-link' href='/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/38f6f/fig5_selections.jpg' style='display: block' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>\n    <span class='gatsby-resp-image-background-image' style=\"padding-bottom: 54%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/jpeg;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;\"></span>\n  <img class='gatsby-resp-image-image' alt='Selected wells under varying risk aversion and budget' title='' src='/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/4b190/fig5_selections.jpg' srcset='/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/e07e9/fig5_selections.jpg 200w,\n/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/066f9/fig5_selections.jpg 400w,\n/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/4b190/fig5_selections.jpg 800w,\n/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/e5166/fig5_selections.jpg 1200w,\n/static/0e0cc4e6fa99bd2ff51ad89b5c026d76/38f6f/fig5_selections.jpg 1366w' sizes='(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px' style='width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;vertical-align:middle;position:absolute;top:0;left:0;' loading='lazy' decoding='async'>\n  </a>\n    </span></p>\n<p><strong>Figure 5.</strong> Optimal well-set diversification as risk aversion <code>lambda</code> and portfolio size <code>n</code> vary.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Impact</h2>\n<p>The method gives asset teams a practical screening tool that preserves risk-awareness, adds optimization certificates (unlike heuristic-only search), and scales to field-sized candidate sets without materially degrading decision quality.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Citation</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bibtex\">@article{farell2026scalable,\n  title   = {Scalable Risk-Averse Well-Placement Optimization Using Quadratic Knapsack Problem and Randomized Singular Value Decomposition},\n  author  = {Farell, R. and Bickel, J. E. and Bajaj, C.},\n  journal = {SPE Journal},\n  volume  = {31},\n  number  = {01},\n  pages   = {480--496},\n  year    = {2026},\n  publisher = {OnePetro}\n}\n</code></pre>","frontmatter":{"title":"Scalable Risk-Averse Well-Placement","slug":"/projects/scalable-risk-averse-well-placement"}}},"pageContext":{"id":"0371c74b-4a1a-5652-a00c-c680b840d264","frontmatter__slug":"/projects/scalable-risk-averse-well-placement","__params":{"frontmatter__slug":"projects"}}},"staticQueryHashes":["3431506307"],"slicesMap":{}}